<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tommaso Campanella</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1568">1568</a>–<a href="/years/1639">1639</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni Domenico Campanella</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tommaso Campanella was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a>, philosopher, theologian, astrologer and poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. He was drawn to the empiricism of Bernardino Telesio and opposed to the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, and was prosecuted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> for heresy in 1594. Accused in 1599 of leading a conspiracy against Spanish rule in Calabria, he was tortured and spent twenty-seven years in prisons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he wrote The City of the Sun, a utopia of a theocratic society with property held in common. Released in 1626 through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a>, he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1634 and spent his last years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tommaso-campanella.jpg" alt="Tommaso Campanella" width="330" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Cozza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cozza_Tommaso_Campanella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Campanella was born into poverty in Stignano in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, the son of an illiterate cobbler, and was a child prodigy. He entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican Order</a> before the age of fourteen, taking the name fra' Tommaso in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>. He became disenchanted with Aristotelian orthodoxy and was drawn to the empiricism of Bernardino Telesio, whom he defended in his first work, Philosophia sensibus demonstrata, published in 1592. His heterodox views brought him into conflict with the ecclesiastical authorities, and he was arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1594 and confined to a convent until 1597.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> he was accused of leading a conspiracy against Spanish rule in his hometown of Stilo. He expected the Age of the Spirit to begin in 1600, on the basis of the prophecies of Joachim of Fiore and his own astrological observations. Betrayed by two fellow conspirators, he was captured in 1599 and tortured on the rack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, seven times in all, and sentenced to life imprisonment. In prison he wrote The Monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1600, a long letter advising Philip II on founding a universal monarchy. The City of the Sun was written in Italian in 1602 and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 1623.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> secured his release in 1626, and he was fully free in 1629. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he advised the pope on astrology for five years. A new conspiracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> in 1634 led by one of his followers threatened fresh trouble, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where Louis XIII received him with favour and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> protected him. He lived in the convent of Saint-Honoré in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> until his death on 21 May 1639.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tommaso Campanella” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
